.TH CVEC_CONSTRUCT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_construct_mp \- declare and create a vector with a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_construct_mp(v, type, mprocs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro declares a vector variable with the name v. That vector holds
elements of the given type. The macro also creates the vector in the same
step. The vector takes every allocation that it makes from mprocs. The
macro is cvec_declare(3) and then cvec_init_mp(3). Everything that those
two macros document holds here unchanged. The macro declares the companion
type variables in the same way, and the other type-dispatching macros read
them. A failed creation stops the program through ccol_fatal_err().

The vector keeps a private copy of the procedure table. The
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t object here therefore does not need to live longer
than the vector. The functions in that table do need to live that long.
The vector frees the backing buffer, the copy of the table and the
container itself through those functions, when it is destroyed. A NULL
mprocs is the same as a call to cvec_construct(3).

The new vector holds no elements. It starts at the minimum capacity of 4.
The capacity doubles when a push finds the buffer full. It halves when the
element count falls below a quarter of it. It never falls below that
minimum of 4.

Destroy the vector with cvec_destroy(3). You can also use
cvec_construct_mp_scoped(3), which destroys the vector automatically at the
end of the scope.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v       Name of the vector variable to declare and create
type    Element type, for example int, double or struct foo
mprocs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t. Its malloc, calloc, realloc
        and free members must all be set. Pass NULL for the default
        allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. The macro expands to declarations and then a statement. It does not
expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when it cannot
create the vector. The message names v and the error below it. Use
cvector_create_full(3) directly to handle a failed creation yourself.
That function does not stop the program.
The macro rejects a procedure table with any of its four members left as
NULL. That rejection is one of the failures that stops the program here.
The macro expands to declarations and then a statement. It therefore
belongs where declarations are legal. It cannot be the body of an if or
a loop without braces.
The macro pastes v into the names of the companion variables. v must
therefore be a plain identifier.
The companion type variables must stay in scope everywhere that you use a
type-dispatching macro on v. cvec_redeclare(3) makes them again for a
handle that comes from another scope.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

cvec_construct_mp(my_vec, int, &procs);
cvec_push(my_vec, 42);
cvec_destroy(my_vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvec_construct (3),
.BR cvec_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR cvec_declare (3),
.BR cvec_init_mp (3),
.BR cvec_redeclare (3),
.BR cvec_destroy (3),
.BR cvector_create_full (3)
